issue.body:
### Describe the situation

A merge that can reduce rows rebuilds a text index from the merged rows, and that rebuild runs in a single builder while the rest of the merge pipeline is already parallel. `BuildTextIndexTransform::aggregate` feeds every block to one `MergeTreeIndexAggregatorText`, so on a `ReplacingMergeTree` the index build, not the merge itself, dominates: on 2,500,000 rows with a 40,000-word vocabulary, `OPTIMIZE FINAL` takes 10.9-11.4 s, of which about 8.5 s is index work (a byte-identical unindexed twin table merges in 2.3-2.5 s). Every engine whose merge may drop rows takes this path: `ReplacingMergeTree`, `CollapsingMergeTree`, TTL deletes and mutations.

The patch adds one `MergeTree` setting, `text_index_build_threads` (default `1`, so behaviour is unchanged unless it is raised). Above 1, blocks are buffered and each of N builders walks all of them, but `MergeTreeIndexTextGranuleBuilder::addToken` keeps only the tokens whose FNV-1a hash selects that builder. The builders therefore partition the **vocabulary**, not the rows: no token is ever held by two builders, no dictionary is duplicated, and each builder emits its own temporary segment, which is combined exactly as a threshold flush already is. Row numbers each builder sees still only increase, which is the order `PostingListBuilder::add` requires, and each builder owns its aggregators, arena and posting lists, so nothing mutable is shared. File writes and flush decisions stay on the transform thread.

Partitioning the same blocks by row range instead was implemented and measured on the same fixture: it is 7.3% slower at 74% more CPU, because each builder then builds nearly the whole 40k dictionary. Partitioning by token hash costs 10.4% more CPU for the 4-way split.

### Which ClickHouse versions are affected?

Reproduced on a build of `master` at `7dc4a129f21a36dd40fecf2198a51db0001b5ae8` (reported version 26.9.1.1). The affected files - `src/Storages/MergeTree/TextIndexUtils.{h,cpp}` and `src/Storages/MergeTree/MergeTreeIndexText.{h,cpp}` - are byte-identical on current `master` (`18544e3557f3da0d60be882855c5b9b619c88c29`), so the single-threaded builder is still the current behaviour.

### How to reproduce

One host, one server, both arms from the same binary and selected only by the setting. 4 cores, arm64.

```sql
-- per arm: p1 (baseline, SETTINGS text_index_build_threads = 1)
--          p4 (candidate, SETTINGS text_index_build_threads = 4)
-- plus a control table with the same rows and no index
CREATE TABLE t (id UInt64, s String,
                INDEX idx s TYPE text(tokenizer = 'default') GRANULARITY 1)
ENGINE = ReplacingMergeTree ORDER BY id
SETTINGS text_index_build_threads = 4;   -- 1 for the baseline arm
```

Data: 5 inserts of 500,000 rows (2,500,000 rows, 5 parts). Each row is 10 space-separated tokens drawn uniformly from a 40,000-word vocabulary of 3-letter Cyrillic words, plus one of the constant markers `code200`..`code204`. Index positions are not enabled.

Timed statement, once per repetition:

```sql
OPTIMIZE TABLE t FINAL SETTINGS optimize_throw_if_noop = 1;
```

Protocol: background merges stopped on every table and enabled only for the table being timed; `system.merges` asserted empty before and after each timed merge; arm order alternated between repetitions; data rebuilt per repetition; wall-clock ms of the timed statement is the statistic. Three paired repetitions in the main run, two more in a second run that also samples `VmRSS`, and three more in a later unchanged re-execution.

Correctness per repetition: the final on-disk text index of the two arms compared byte for byte; 16 needles (`code200`..`code204` plus 11 vocabulary words) queried through each merged index and compared against the same query on the unindexed twin; row counts compared across arms before and after the merge.

### Expected performance

`OPTIMIZE FINAL`, wall-clock ms, 4 builders vs 1. Run 1 = `optimize_final_replacing_2p5m_run1`, run 2 = `optimize_final_replacing_2p5m_run2_rss` (same harness plus a 50 ms `VmRSS` sampler), run 3 = `optimize_final_replacing_2p5m_run3_repro` (unchanged re-execution on a later day):

| repetition | `text_index_build_threads = 1` | `= 4` | ratio |
|---|---|---|---|
| run 1, rep 1 | 11407 | 6927 | 0.607 |
| run 1, rep 2 | 11052 | 6849 | 0.620 |
| run 1, rep 3 | 11082 | 7040 | 0.635 |
| run 2, rep 1 | 11303 | 7457 | 0.660 |
| run 2, rep 2 | 11244 | 7095 | 0.631 |
| run 3, rep 1 | 11824 | 7037 | 0.595 |
| run 3, rep 2 | 11191 | 7010 | 0.626 |
| run 3, rep 3 | 11027 | 7194 | 0.652 |

Run 1: paired geometric ratio 0.621, i.e. **-37.9%**, 95% CI -41.3% to -34.4%. Run 3, an unchanged re-execution of the same harness on a later day: 0.624, i.e. **-37.6%**, 95% CI -44.3% to -30.0%. Run 2 carries a 50 ms `VmRSS` sampler, so both of its arms read higher; its ratios are 0.660 and 0.631. The candidate is faster in all eight repetitions, arm ranges are disjoint within each run, and arm order alternates between repetitions. The unindexed control table merges in 2504/2508/2519 ms (run 1) and 2517/2481/2532 ms (run 3), so the index's share of the merge falls from about 8.6 s to about 4.4 s.

Correctness and costs measured with the same runs:

- Final on-disk index **byte-identical** to the serial build in 5 of 5 repetitions; 0 needle failures; row counts invariant (59,098,837 -> 45,118,135 per repetition).
- Stateless suites: `text_index` (211 tests) and `full_text` (3) run with the setting at 1, at 4, and on an unpatched binary. Failing test **names** are the same set in all three arms (175/211 OK, 36 pre-existing failures in this sandbox, `full_text` 3/3 OK), so the setting introduces no failure.
- Server CPU for the merge: 11.3-12.1 s -> 12.6-12.9 s (+10.4% in run 1). The win is wall-clock, not work.
- Temporary segments per merge: 3 -> 12.
- Peak resident memory of the merging server, sampled every 50 ms across the timed merge, shows no consistent direction over four repetitions: -3.4%, +8.5%, +0.6%, +2.6%. The unindexed control's own peak moves 6.6% between repetitions, so at this sample size the arm difference is bounded, not resolved. Structurally there is nothing to multiply: the final index is byte-identical, so the vocabulary is partitioned rather than copied, and the patch divides `text_index_max_processed_tokens_before_flush` and `text_index_max_memory_usage_before_flush` by the thread count. The two RSS-sampled repetitions reproduced the speedup again (11150 -> 7189 ms and 11291 -> 7105 ms).
- Under a concurrent read load the merge still wins, by less: with two clients continuously running `SELECT count() FROM qt WHERE hasToken(message, 'обычный')` against a separate text-indexed table (1,000,000 rows, 16 parts) for the whole merge, the merge takes 13313/13627 ms with one builder and 10280/10371 ms with four (-22.8%, -23.9%) - four builders now compete with the readers for the same 4 cores. The readers' own server-side latency is the same in both arms at the median (7 ms, against 4 ms with no merge running) and the p95 bands overlap (13.0/12.6 ms serial, 12/16 ms sharded, about 200-290 queries per arm), but the worst single observation is higher with four builders: 32 and 27 ms versus 21 and 18 ms. Treat that tail as the cost of raising the setting on a server that is also serving queries.
- Insert path unaffected (median of 15 inserts per arm: 1221 vs 1217 ms in run 1, 1247 vs 1231 ms in run 3; unindexed 537-540 ms) and read latency on the merged index unchanged (2 ms in both arms, 6 repetitions per run).

Limitations: peak memory is bounded but not precisely resolved, as above; the concurrency check used two client threads on the same 4 cores, so more clients, several simultaneous merges each claiming four builders, and hosts with more cores are unmeasured; one host with 4 cores, so a 4-way split saturates it and wider splits are unmeasured; one tokenizer (`default`), one 40k-token corpus, one 2.5M-row scale, warm page cache. The fixture does not enable index positions, so phrase search built by more than one builder is untested - that is the main gap before the setting could ever default above 1. Each builder's flush budget is the configured budget divided by the thread count, which keeps peak memory close to serial but makes each segment smaller. A plain `MergeTree` merge is unaffected: it merges the existing dictionaries and posting lists instead of rebuilding them, and never reaches this transform.

