Thanks. I applied `d8dc5cdb` on master `2170ef3` and reran it against MongoDB 6.0 next to a master build, through the `mongodb` table function and the `MongoDB` engine (identical results), for `Bool` and `Nullable(Bool)`. Besides your two layouts I used a collection mixing BSON bool, int32, int64 and double in one column, and one with NULLs, and checked every count against the same rows copied into a `Memory` table.

Both points are fixed: your counts reproduce, and the two-encoding lists and `$or` ranges return the right rows in every layout, including `!=` / `NOT IN`, inverted ranges, an `Int32` column compared with `true`, and `AND` / `OR`. What is still wrong is the same-type case you describe (`flag = true`, `n = 1`, `n > 0` on the other encoding), as on master.

One minor thing: `flag = 2` and `flag != 2` now throw `Code: 48` (with the default `mongodb_throw_on_unsupported_query = 1`), while master returns the right counts for `Bool` with `{"$eq": 2}` / `{"$ne": 2}`, and `flag NOT IN (2)` works here. For an integer constant that has no `Bool` value, `=` and `!=` can keep the constant in its own BSON type instead of refusing:

```cpp
const bool keep_own_encoding = converted_value.isNull() && isBoolNumberComparison(const_type, column_type)
    && isInteger(removeNullable(const_type)) && (func_name == "$eq" || func_name == "$ne");
if (converted_value.isNull() && !keep_own_encoding)
{
    // LOG_DEBUG + return {} as now
}
if (!keep_own_encoding)
{
    // other_encodings, const_type, const_value as now
}
```

With that, `flag = 2`, `flag != 2`, `flag = -1` and `flag != 256` return the right counts in every layout, also for `Nullable(Bool)` (master pushes `{"$ne": true}` there and returns 2 rows instead of 4), no other predicate changes, and `flag != 0.5` stays refused.

